WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.717
Welcome to week seven, the last week of new material.

2
00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:07.485
The theme: more agents, more autonomy, more ways to fail.

3
00:00:07.535 --> 00:00:15.774
This video is about multi-agent systems, which are fashionable, sometimes right, and more often a way to make a simple problem hard.

4
00:00:16.524 --> 00:00:19.328
Multi-agent systems come in two main shapes.

5
00:00:19.378 --> 00:00:29.522
An orchestrator with workers: one lead agent breaks the job up, sends pieces to worker agents, each with its own context, and assembles the results.

6
00:00:29.572 --> 00:00:40.124
And a handoff chain: the task moves from one specialized agent to the next, triage to expert to closer, each with its own instructions and tools.

7
00:00:40.174 --> 00:00:47.056
Both exist because one context window cannot hold everything, and because specialized instructions beat one giant prompt.

8
00:00:47.106 --> 00:00:50.318
Both also multiply the ways things go wrong.

9
00:00:51.068 --> 00:00:52.926
Here is the case for.

10
00:00:52.976 --> 00:01:02.467
In June 2025 Anthropic described its research system: a lead agent that plans and spawns sub-agents to search in parallel, each with a fresh context.

11
00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:09.718
On their internal research evaluation it beat a single agent using the same top model by about 90 percent.

12
00:01:09.768 --> 00:01:11.245
It is not cheap.

13
00:01:11.295 --> 00:01:18.496
Agents already use several times the tokens of a chat, and this system used roughly fifteen times as many.

14
00:01:18.546 --> 00:01:25.365
Their own conclusion was that the pattern pays off when the task is wide, parallel and valuable enough.

15
00:01:25.415 --> 00:01:26.892
That describes open-ended research.

16
00:01:26.942 --> 00:01:30.708
It does not describe a form that needs filling in.

17
00:01:31.458 --> 00:01:35.014
Here is the case against, published one day earlier.

18
00:01:35.064 --> 00:01:45.830
Cognition, the company behind the Devin coding agent, argued against building multi-agent systems for work that produces one coherent thing, such as a codebase or a document.

19
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:54.242
Sub-agents that each see part of the context make decisions that conflict, and the conflicts are plausible enough to slip through.

20
00:01:54.292 --> 00:02:05.860
Their two principles: share the full context, including the history of decisions, and remember that every action carries an implicit decision that the next agent needs to know about.

21
00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.263
The two posts are not contradictory.

22
00:02:08.313 --> 00:02:09.064
Search parallelises.

23
00:02:09.114 --> 00:02:10.266
Construction does not.

24
00:02:11.016 --> 00:02:18.737
Researchers at Berkeley catalogued how multi-agent systems fail, in a paper first posted in March 2025.

25
00:02:18.787 --> 00:02:27.964
They read 150 traces from seven popular frameworks, with expert annotators, and found fourteen failure modes in three groups.

26
00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:33.306
Specification and design: unclear roles, missing stop conditions, violated task specifications.

27
00:02:33.356 --> 00:02:39.134
Inter-agent misalignment: agents ignoring each other, withholding information, talking past each other.

28
00:02:39.184 --> 00:02:43.990
And task verification: agents declaring work done without checking it.

29
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.418
Most failures were design failures.

30
00:02:46.468 --> 00:02:51.275
The same lesson as week four, now with more agents.

31
00:02:52.025 --> 00:02:55.363
So before your team adds a second agent, five questions.

32
00:02:55.413 --> 00:02:57.057
Would a workflow step do?

33
00:02:57.107 --> 00:03:01.123
Routing and fixed sub-tasks usually do not need an agent at all.

34
00:03:01.173 --> 00:03:05.188
Is the work parallel, like search, or one artifact, like a report?

35
00:03:05.238 --> 00:03:09.254
What does each agent see, and who holds the record of decisions?

36
00:03:09.304 --> 00:03:15.014
How will you evaluate the whole system and each part, with the golden set you already have?

37
00:03:15.064 --> 00:03:20.096
And what does it cost per task compared with the single agent you have now?

38
00:03:20.146 --> 00:03:24.500
For most capstone projects the honest answer is one agent with good tools.

39
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.566
Next video: agents that run for hours, write code and use computers.
